```kotlin
override fun onCleared() {
    super.onCleared()
    synchronized(analyzerLock) {
        analyzerClosed = true
        analyzer?.close()
        analyzer = null
    }
}
```
The doc on line 102 states the intent explicitly: "Serializes [analyzeGuarded] against [onCleared]: close waits for in-flight analysis." `onCleared()` runs on the main thread (ViewModelStore.clear from the NavBackStackEntry), while `analyzeGuarded` holds the same monitor on `ioDispatcher` (line 345 `synchronized(analyzerLock)`) for the whole of `ImageQualityAnalyzer.analyze()` — a full `getPixels` of up to 2,000,000 pixels (`MAX_ANALYSIS_PIXELS`, line 603), a 2M-iteration luma pass, a 2M-iteration Laplacian pass (`analyzeBlur`, ImageQualityAnalyzer.kt:88-102) and a MediaPipe `handLandmarker.detect` call. Backing out of the scan screen while an angle is being analysed therefore parks the main thread on a monitor for the remainder of that analysis.

fix:
Keep the close serialized against in-flight analysis, but never acquire analyzerLock from the main thread. Set the @Volatile flag inline (it already short-circuits analyzeGuarded at line 346 without the lock) and hand the actual native close to the IO dispatcher.

Replace ScanViewModel.kt:314-321 with:

```kotlin
override fun onCleared() {
    super.onCleared()
    // @Volatile: any analyzeGuarded that has not yet entered the monitor bails out here.
    analyzerClosed = true
    // viewModelScope is already cancelled by ViewModel.clear() at this point, and the
    // in-flight analyze() is uninterruptible CPU/JNI work, so the close must wait for the
    // monitor on a background thread rather than parking the UI thread on it.
    CoroutineScope(SupervisorJob() + ioDispatcher).launch {
        synchronized(analyzerLock) {
            analyzer?.close()
            analyzer = null
        }
    }
}
```

